WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:06.399
My name is Neerja Aggarwal and I was an electrical
engineering and theater arts major in my undergraduate here.

00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:11.190
And now I’m studying electrical engineering
as a master’s student.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:16.570
Last spring is when I started having discussions
about possibly directing Einstein’s Dreams.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:20.830
It was my undergraduate theater thesis project.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:24.970
The full spring production for MIT Theater
Arts this year was going to be directed by me,

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:30.400
which is a huge responsibility because it’s usually a role that’s taken on by faculty members or outside directors.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:37.200
So I was both lucky and very empowered by
the entire theater department to take on this responsibility.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.590
There were definitely times where I was not
making decisions fast enough or I wasn’t

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:46.579
really quite sure what I wanted, and there
was still a lot of learning on the job as

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:47.579
I went through.

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:50.710
I mean, MIT’s philosophy is Mens et Manus,
right?

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.649
So it’s Mind and Hand, and these were really
more growing opportunities, where you’re

00:00:54.649 --> 00:01:00.559
working on a show, but you realize that you’re
learning life skills instead.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.460
Einstein’s Dreams is about these different
worlds where time runs differently.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.970
It explores the human ramifications of this
slight manipulation in time.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:19.659
So imagine a world where our lifetimes just
are circular, they keep repeating themselves.

00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:23.689
What effect does that have on the people living
out their lives in this circular time?

00:01:23.689 --> 00:01:28.140
What effect does it have when they slowly
start to realize that they’re carrying out

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.930
the same loop over and over again, and they
can’t break out of it?

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:37.219
It’s also about Einstein’s creative journey
toward discovering special relativity.

00:01:37.219 --> 00:01:39.960
So, in a way, it’s his thought experiments.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.829
It’s him sitting down and thinking, “Ok,
well what if time worked like this?

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:45.440
Then what would happen?”

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.829
In this show, there’s not a single Einstein.

00:01:47.829 --> 00:01:52.480
Anybody could be Einstein, because this role
is transitioning through all these three different

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:53.480
actresses.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.619
Sabrine Iqbal, she’s a course 2 senior.

00:01:56.619 --> 00:02:02.500
Alexa Garcia is a course 20 senior, and Alexandra
Boles, she’s studying political science

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:03.969
at Wellesley.

00:02:03.969 --> 00:02:09.380
Probing deeper, I realized that for me, what
I was interested in was the cost of creativity,

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.860
the struggles, the frustrations, the obsessions
of the creative process,

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.510
and that’s what Einstein’s Dreams is also
about.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:21.040
And that’s something I feel like we all
at MIT here can relate to.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:26.069
Pretty much anything that’s open-ended and
creative and has never been done before, you

00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:29.360
go through this very similar journey.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.980
As a director, you’re leading the actors
but then you’re also collaborating with

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:43.129
all of the designers and leading that team,
talking back and forth, exchanging ideas,

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:46.099
you know, posing questions and seeing the response.

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:50.269
Your job really is to set the tone of the
room, and create a safe environment where

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:51.569
accidents can happen.

00:02:51.569 --> 00:02:56.580
Because sometimes, you know, an accident is
the most brilliant part of a show.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:01.599
In a way, directing a show is very similar
to doing research, and that’s interesting

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:04.800
because then you can use what you’ve learned
from one and apply it to the other.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:10.230
So, for example, some tools and activities
that I’ve learned in directing — how to

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:16.040
brainstorm ideas, how to build off of a problem
statement, how to curate different ideas — I’ve

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.810
actually been able to use that in my graduate
research also, to help me think about problems,

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:22.240
how to break things down.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:27.670
It’s really until you’re in the rehearsal
room, making decisions on the job, when you

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:33.000
actually learn what it is to make work with
a group of other people.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.400
I decided I wanted to be an engineer because
I felt that being an engineer would give me

00:03:38.409 --> 00:03:42.220
the skills and capabilities to work on really
difficult problems.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:46.220
But people are also half of that equation,
so how do you communicate those results?

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:52.209
How do you turn that discovery into an impact?

00:03:52.209 --> 00:03:57.020
That was my inspiration for studying both
engineering and theater here at MIT.

